Internal Working of transaction:
=================================
@Transactional in Spring is a powerful annotation that manages database transactions automatically. Here’s how it works 
under the hood:

✅ 1. What It Does

Wraps your method execution in a transaction boundary.
Ensures ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability).
Handles commit or rollback based on success or exceptions.


✅ 2. How It Works Internally

When Spring sees @Transactional on a method or class:

Bean Post-Processor detects it and creates a proxy (similar to AOP).
The proxy intercepts method calls and delegates to a TransactionInterceptor.
The interceptor:

Starts a transaction before the method executes.
Executes the method.
Commits if successful, or rolls back if an exception occurs (based on rollback rules).

✅ 3. Transaction Manager

Spring uses a PlatformTransactionManager (e.g., DataSourceTransactionManager for JDBC, JpaTransactionManager for JPA).
This manager interacts with the underlying database connection.


✅ 4. Important Details

Propagation: Defines,/u how transactions behave when calling another transactional method (e.g., REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW).
Isolation: Controls how concurrent transactions interact (e.g., READ_COMMITTED, SERIALIZABLE).
Rollback Rules: By default, rolls back on unchecked exceptions (RuntimeException), not on checked exceptions unless configured.


✅ 5. Self-Invocation Issue

If you call a @Transactional method from another method in the same class, the proxy is bypassed → transaction won’t start.
Solution: Use separate beans or AspectJ mode.   
