Transaction Propogation:
-----------------------
Transaction propogation referes to how transaction behaves when a method annotated with @transactional called by a method
that may or may not have a active transaction.

✅ Propagation Types in Spring
Spring provides several propagation options via Propagation enum:


REQUIRED (default)

If a transaction exists, join it.
If none exists, create a new one.
Most commonly used.



REQUIRES_NEW

Always start a new transaction.
Suspends the existing transaction if present.
Useful when you want isolation from the caller’s transaction.



SUPPORTS

If a transaction exists, join it.
If none exists, run without a transaction.
Good for read-only operations.

MANDATORY

Must run inside an existing transaction.
Throws TransactionRequiredException if none exists.

NOT_SUPPORTED

Never run inside a transaction.
Suspends any existing transaction.

NEVER

Must run without a transaction.
Throws an exception if a transaction exists.

NESTED

Creates a nested transaction within the existing one using savepoints.
Rollback of nested transaction doesn’t affect the outer transaction.
Works only with JDBC and DataSourceTransactionManager.

By default it required.
When to use Required.


WorkFlow:
1. REQUIRED (Default)

Behavior: If a transaction exists, join it; if not, create a new one.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Callee joins same transaction.
Caller has no transaction → Callee starts a new transaction.


Use Case: Most common scenario.


2. REQUIRES_NEW

Behavior: Always start a new transaction, suspending any existing one.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Suspend it → Start new transaction for callee → Commit/Rollback → Resume caller’s transaction.


Use Case: Independent work that should not affect caller’s transaction.


3. SUPPORTS

Behavior: If a transaction exists, join it; if not, execute non-transactionally.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Callee joins.
Caller has no transaction → Callee runs without transaction.


Use Case: Read-only operations.


4. MANDATORY

Behavior: Must run inside an existing transaction; throws exception if none exists.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Callee joins.
Caller has no transaction → Exception thrown.


Use Case: Enforce transactional context.


5. NOT_SUPPORTED

Behavior: Always run non-transactionally; suspends any existing transaction.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Suspend → Callee runs without transaction → Resume caller’s transaction.


Use Case: Operations that must not run in a transaction (e.g., long-running tasks).


6. NEVER

Behavior: Must run without a transaction; throws exception if one exists.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Exception thrown.
Caller has no transaction → Callee runs normally.


Use Case: Explicitly forbid transactions.


7. NESTED

Behavior: If a transaction exists, create a nested transaction (savepoint); else, create a new one.
Workflow:

Caller has transaction → Callee creates savepoint → Rollback only nested part if needed.
Caller has no transaction → Callee starts new transaction.


Use Case: Partial rollback within a larger transaction.
