-> Isolation:Transactions run independantly and should not interfere with each other.
-> Intermediate states of a transaction are not visible to other transactions

Isolation in databases refers to one of the ACID properties that ensures transactions do not interfere with each other. 
It controls how and when the changes made by one transaction become visible to other concurrent transactions.

In a multi-user environment, multiple transactions run at the same time.
Without isolation, you can face issues like:

Dirty Reads:A dirty read happens when a transaction reads data that has been modified by another transaction but not yet 
committed. If the second transaction rolls back, the first transaction has read invalid data.

Example Scenario
Imagine two transactions in a banking system:

Transaction A: Updates account_balance from $1000 to $500 (withdraws $500).
Transaction B: Reads account_balance while Transaction A is still in progress (before commit).

Steps:

Transaction A starts and updates the balance to $500 but hasn't committed yet.
Transaction B reads the balance as $500 and uses it for some calculation (e.g., approving a loan).
Transaction A rolls back due to an error, restoring the balance to $1000.
Transaction B has made decisions based on incorrect data ($500), which is a dirty read.


Non Repeatable Read;
====================
A non-repeatable read occurs when a transaction reads the same row twice and gets different values because another 
transaction modified and committed the data in between.

Telecom Postpaid CRM Example
Imagine two transactions:

Transaction A (Customer Care Agent): Viewing a customer’s outstanding bill during a call.
Transaction B (Billing System): Updates the customer’s bill after adding roaming charges.


Steps

Transaction A reads outstanding_amount = ₹1,000 for customer John.
Transaction B adds roaming charges and commits the update:
outstanding_amount = ₹1,500.
Transaction A reads the same row again (maybe to confirm before payment) and now sees ₹1,500.

The two reads in Transaction A gave different results → Non-repeatable Read.

Why It Happens

Occurs in READ COMMITTED isolation level.
Prevented by REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE, which lock rows for the duration of the transaction.

A phantom read happens when a transaction executes the same query twice and gets different sets of rows because another transaction inserted or deleted rows in between.

Telecom CRM Phantom Read Example
Imagine two transactions:

Transaction A (Customer Care Agent): Fetches all postpaid customers with outstanding bills > ₹1,000 to send reminders.
Transaction B (Billing System): Inserts a new record for a customer who just got billed ₹2,000.

In spring boot application we make use of @Transactional(propogation=, isolation=isolation.) In this way, we declare it.
Isolation levels:
1. Default.
2. Read uncomitted.
3. Read Comitted.
4. Repeatable Read.
5. Serializable.
=================================================================================
Read Comitted,

1. When Transaction A updates a row, that change is not visible to other transactions until A commits.
Transaction B, operating under READ COMMITTED, will only see the last committed value, not the uncommitted one.

2. Repeatable Read:

Goal: Prevent dirty reads and non-repeatable reads.
How: When a transaction reads a row, it locks that row so no other transaction can modify it until the first one finishes.
Example (Telecom CRM):

Transaction A: Reads customer’s outstanding bill = ₹1,000.
Transaction B: Tries to update bill to ₹1,500 → blocked until A completes.
So A will always see ₹1,000 during its transaction → no non-repeatable read.


Still allows: Phantom reads (new rows can appear if another transaction inserts).
3. Goal: Prevent dirty reads, non-repeatable reads, and phantom reads.
How: Treats transactions as if they run one after another (full range locks).
Example (Telecom CRM):

Transaction A: Queries all customers with bills > ₹1,000.
Transaction B: Tries to insert a new customer with ₹2,000 bill → blocked until A finishes.
So A sees a consistent set of rows → no phantom read.

